Appendix F: Evaluation Specifications

F.1 Test 1: Bottleneck Identification Accuracy

Protocol.

· Sample size: 50 cases
· Expert panel: 3 blinded expert caseworkers with ≥5 years experience
· Procedure: Each panelist independently ranks the top 3 bottlenecks for each case
· Primary outcome: Top-1 agreement between AI and panel majority
· Secondary outcome: Spearman rank correlation across top 3

Thresholds.

· Top-1 agreement ≥ 70%
· Spearman rank correlation ≥ 0.6
· False bottleneck rate (bottlenecks judged irrelevant or harmful) < 5%

Justification. Caseworkers disagree with each other; 80% agreement with a single caseworker is not a meaningful bar. A blinded panel with 70% majority agreement is achievable and clinically meaningful. The 5% false bottleneck threshold protects against harmful recommendations.

Power analysis. With 50 cases and a true agreement rate of 70%, the 95% confidence interval is approximately [56%, 84%]. This is sufficient to detect a meaningful difference from the null hypothesis of 50% agreement (random chance).

F.2 Test 2: Time-to-Stabilization

Protocol.

· Design: Stepped-wedge cluster randomized trial
· Sites: 4–6 Continuums of Care
· Primary outcome: Time from first contact to stable housing (30 consecutive days in permanent housing)
· Analysis: Survival models with intention-to-treat
· Baseline: 150 days to stabilization (documented in prior studies)

Threshold. 15% reduction in time-to-stabilization (approximately 22.5 days on a 150-day baseline), with p < 0.05.

Power analysis. With 6 sites, 20 individuals per site per period, and 4 periods, the total sample size is 480. Assuming an intra-cluster correlation of 0.05 and a 15% effect size, the study has 80% power to detect the effect at \alpha = 0.05. This calculation uses the standard formula for stepped-wedge designs.

Stepped-wedge design. The design is a stepped-wedge cluster randomized trial across multiple sites, where the intervention is rolled out sequentially to different sites over time. This design is preferred when the intervention cannot be delivered simultaneously to all sites due to resource constraints.

F.3 Test 3: Trajectory Durability

Protocol.

· Sample: All individuals who stabilized in Test 2
· Primary outcome: Return to homelessness within 12 months
· Analysis: Logistic regression with site fixed effects
· Baseline: Documented return rate in the target population

Threshold. 10 percentage point reduction in 12-month return rate, with p < 0.05.

Justification. CTI effects on homelessness at follow-up are small; durability at 12 months is a higher bar. A 10 percentage point reduction is the minimum clinically significant difference that would justify continuation.

F.4 Test 4: Dignity, Consent, and Autonomy

Protocol.

· Design: Mixed methods
· Quantitative: Validated scales for autonomy, trust, and perceived surveillance
· Qualitative: Semi-structured interviews with 20 participants (including opt-outs)
· Pre-registration: Instrument pre-registered before data collection

Thresholds.

· No more than 10% of users report feeling surveilled, judged, or coerced
· Median score on autonomy subscale at or above midpoint

Justification. The dignity constraint is architectural, not aspirational. If more than one in ten users experiences the system as coercive, the ethical architecture has failed regardless of quantitative outcomes.

F.5 Process Metrics

Metric Definition Target
Time from first message to first referral Elapsed time < 48 hours
Referral completion rate % referrals closed with tracked outcome 60%
Data staleness Median age of capacity data at query time < 4 hours
False bottleneck rate Bottlenecks judged irrelevant or harmful < 5%
User retention through first stabilization step % users who complete first intervention 50%
Cost per successful trajectory initiation Total cost / number of stabilizations < $5,000

F.6 Power Analysis Details

For Test 2 (stepped-wedge cluster randomized trial):

· Number of sites: 6
· Number of periods: 4
· Individuals per site per period: 20
· Total sample size: 6 × 4 × 20 = 480
· Intra-cluster correlation (ICC): 0.05
· Design effect: 1 + (20 - 1) × 0.05 = 1.95
· Effective sample size: 480 / 1.95 = 246
· Minimum detectable effect: 15% reduction in time-to-stabilization
· Power: 80%
· Significance level: 0.05

This calculation uses the standard formula for stepped-wedge designs. The ICC of 0.05 is conservative for homeless services research, where clustering is typically weak because individuals within a site have heterogeneous trajectories.

F.7 Pre-Registration Requirements

The following must be pre-registered before data collection:

· Primary and secondary outcomes
· Analysis plan (including subgroup analyses)
· Sample size and power calculations
· Stopping rules
· Handling of missing data
· Ethical approvals

Registration will be on ClinicalTrials.gov or an equivalent registry. Pre-registration prevents post-hoc revision of hypotheses and strengthens the credibility of the evaluation.

